  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\nThe article argues that AI agent threats aren't hype, but rather emerge from two compounding capability curves—agent endurance (task duration) and orchestration breadth (sub-agent coordination)—both of which are growing rapidly in production systems. The author uses the Terminator franchise as a framing device, reinterpreting \"it absolutely will not stop\" as \"it absolutely will not stop billing,\" and highlights LLMjacking (stolen cloud credentials running AI workloads on the victim's dime) as the economic mechanism that severs the link between capability and cost. The core security insight is that defenders face per-attempt costs (human review) while attackers face near-zero costs (automated swarm on stolen compute), making aggregate success inevitable regardless of per-attempt failure rates. The tone is analytical with cinematic framing",
